+// Fling-throttle: while the scrolling thread reports a fast fling (see
+// ScrollingTree::flingThrottleActive), cap main-thread rendering updates to at most
+// one per this many milliseconds. Page::updateRendering is where scroll DOM events,
+// rAF callbacks, IntersectionObserver notifications, style recalc, layout and paint
+// all run, so this single gate starves scroll-triggered page work during the fling
+// and lets the compositor thread move already-painted tiles at full rate; the page
+// catches up on settle. Deliberately lossy: lazy-loaded content, scroll-linked JS
+// and sticky-header class toggles lag until the fling settles.
+//
+// SAFETY (the builds-465..471 lesson): the RenderingUpdate RunLoopObserver is
+// Type::Repeating and is our only self-healing wakeup — this gate must NEVER
+// invalidate or defer its scheduling. When throttled we simply return without
+// running updateRendering(), leaving the observer scheduled so it re-fires on every
+// main-loop iteration, and arm a one-shot timer for the interval remainder so a
+// quiet run loop still wakes up to deliver the final update. The
+// m_isWaitingForRenderer handshake is untouched.
+//
+// WEBKIT_FLING_THROTTLE_MS: unset/0 = disabled (stock); N = minimum ms between
+// rendering updates during a fling (e.g. 200 = 5Hz). Detector knobs
+// (WEBKIT_FLING_THROTTLE_SPEED / _SETTLE_MS) live in ScrollingTree.cpp.
+static double flingThrottleIntervalMs()
+{
+    static double ms = [] {
+        if (const char* env = getenv("WEBKIT_FLING_THROTTLE_MS")) {
+            double v = atof(env);
+            if (v > 0 && v <= 1000)
+                return v;
+        }
+        return 0.0;
+    }();
+    return ms;
+}
